\newlabel{fig:f1bar}{{4}{8}{F1 score on the positive (\texttt {died}) class. The ranking is strikingly different from accuracy: SMO ($\fOneSMO $), OneR ($\fOneOneR $) and IBk1 ($\fOneIBkOne $) lead, while ZeroR collapses to F1$=0$ despite scoring $\accZeroR \%$ accuracy. Same colour scheme as Figure~\ref {fig:accbar}}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}Precision--recall trade-off}{8}{subsection.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Precision--recall scatter on \texttt  {died}. Iso-F1 contours ($F_1\!\in \!\{0.40,0.50,0.60\}$) are drawn dashed. \textbf  {MyDT+} sits alone at the high-precision end ($P=0.660$): of the patients it predicts will die, $66\%$ actually do, the highest positive predictive value in the entire study. \textbf  {IBk\,$k{=}1$}, \textbf  {SMO} and the unpruned trees occupy the high-recall end. \textbf  {Bagging J48} dominates no other classifier and is dominated by SMO, J48-pruned and MyDT+ on every metric; the scatter makes the ``ensembles do not help'' finding visible at a glance.}}{8}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:prscatter}{{5}{8}{Precision--recall scatter on \texttt {died}. Iso-F1 contours ($F_1\!\in \!\{0.40,0.50,0.60\}$) are drawn dashed. \textbf {MyDT+} sits alone at the high-precision end ($P=\precDTplus $): of the patients it predicts will die, $66\%$ actually do, the highest positive predictive value in the entire study. \textbf {IBk\,$k{=}1$}, \textbf {SMO} and the unpruned trees occupy the high-recall end. \textbf {Bagging J48} dominates no other classifier and is dominated by SMO, J48-pruned and MyDT+ on every metric; the scatter makes the ``ensembles do not help'' finding visible at a glance}{figure.caption.8}{}}
